+  <div class="wrap">
+    <h2>The management fee in dollars, at Myrtle Beach rents</h2>
+    <p>Myrtle Beach's median asking rent is about $1,726/mo (Zillow Observed Rent Index, 2026-05, Myrtle Beach metro area). Managers in the US typically charge 8-12% of collected rent. Here is what that percentage costs per year before any add-on fees, next to RenPro's flat rate.</p>
+    <div class="tbl-scroll"><table class="cmp">
+      <tr><th>Portfolio</th><th>At 8%</th><th>At 10%</th><th>At 12%</th><th class="hl">RenPro software</th></tr>
+      <tr><td><b>1 unit</b></td><td>$1,657/yr</td><td>$2,071/yr</td><td>$2,485/yr</td><td class="hl good">$1,788/yr flat</td></tr><tr><td><b>5 units</b></td><td>$8,285/yr</td><td>$10,356/yr</td><td>$12,427/yr</td><td class="hl good">$1,788/yr flat</td></tr><tr><td><b>10 units</b></td><td>$16,570/yr</td><td>$20,712/yr</td><td>$24,854/yr</td><td class="hl good">$1,788/yr flat</td></tr><tr><td><b>20 units</b></td><td>$33,139/yr</td><td>$41,424/yr</td><td>$49,709/yr</td><td class="hl good">$1,788/yr flat</td></tr><tr><td><b>50 units</b></td><td>$82,848/yr</td><td>$103,560/yr</td><td>$124,272/yr</td><td class="hl good">$1,788/yr flat</td></tr>
+    </table></div>
+    <p style="font-size:13px;margin-top:10px">Management percentages are national ranges (sources below); individual Myrtle Beach firms quote above and below them. RenPro is software, not a management company: it automates the office work and you or your team handle showings and inspections.</p>
+  </div>
+</section><section>
+  <div class="wrap">
+    <h2>The fees beyond the monthly percentage</h2>
+    <p>The monthly percentage is the advertised number. Most management contracts add several more line items, and they apply in Myrtle Beach the same way they do everywhere else:</p>
+    <div class="cards"><div class="card"><div class="t">Tenant placement / leasing fee</div><div class="s"><b style="color:var(--text)">50-100% of first month&#039;s rent (iPropertyManagement measured average: 70.6% of one month&#039;s rent when bundled with management; 81.8% for placement-only; flat-rate alternative averages $555.53 with management, $670.23 placement-only).</b> Charged each time a new tenant is placed. Covers marketing, showings, screening, lease prep, move-in. This is the single biggest add-on and it recurs with every turnover, so high-turnover properties pay it repeatedly.</div></div><div class="card"><div class="t">Lease renewal fee</div><div class="s"><b style="color:var(--text)">$200-$500 flat (iPropertyManagement measured average: $211.92; Baselane cites $100-$300 or a % of one month&#039;s rent).</b> Charged when an EXISTING tenant simply re-signs - i.e., a fee for paperwork on a tenant the owner already has. Charged annually on every renewal.</div></div><div class="card"><div class="t">Maintenance markup / coordination fee</div><div class="s"><b style="color:var(--text)">10-15% added to vendor invoices is the most-cited range (sources span 5-25%).</b> A surcharge on top of the actual repair bill for coordinating the vendor. On $3,000/yr of repairs a 15% markup is $450/yr of pure fee. Some firms instead use in-house crews at above-market hourly rates.</div></div><div class="card"><div class="t">Vacancy fee</div><div class="s"><b style="color:var(--text)">$50-$150 per month while the unit sits empty; some contracts charge one month&#039;s rent equivalent or keep billing the full management fee (&#039;rent due&#039; contracts).</b> Charged for watching an empty unit (inspections, lawn, marketing). Stings because it hits exactly when the owner has zero income. Verify whether the management fee is on &#039;rent collected&#039; or &#039;rent due&#039; - the latter means full fees during vacancy.</div></div><div class="card"><div class="t">Setup / onboarding fee</div><div class="s"><b style="color:var(--text)">$200-$500 one-time per property (most sources cluster at $300-$500).</b> One-time charge to open the account: bookkeeping setup, initial inspection, registration help. Charged before the PM has collected a dollar of rent.</div></div><div class="card"><div class="t">Eviction coordination fee</div><div class="s"><b style="color:var(--text)">$200-$500 for the PM&#039;s time (DoorLoop cites $300-$1,000+), PLUS court filing fees and attorney costs billed to the owner.</b> Covers serving notices, court appearances, coordinating turnover. The PM fee is on top of, not instead of, actual legal costs.</div></div><div class="card"><div class="t">Early termination fee</div><div class="s"><b style="color:var(--text)">Flat $50-$1,000, or roughly one month&#039;s management fee or more; some contracts demand all remaining management fees for the lease/contract term.</b> Charged for firing the manager before the contract ends. Contracts typically also require 30-60 day notice, and some allow the PM to hold owner funds (including the reserve) for 30-60 days after termination to cover trailing expenses.</div></div><div class="card"><div class="t">Inspection fees</div><div class="s"><b style="color:var(--text)">$75-$300 per inspection (iPropertyManagement measured average: $106.72).</b> Move-in/move-out inspections are often bundled, but periodic or extra inspections (drive-by, annual interior) are frequently billed separately per visit.</div></div></div>
+    <p>Industry blog analyses converge on a real all-in cost well above the advertised percentage: ClearLead Digital&#039;s 2026 fee analysis says the monthly fee is only one of 5-8 fees most managers charge and total FIRST-YEAR cost typically runs 18-20% of gross rent; the same and related guides advise budgeting 12-15% of gross rent even when the base rate is 8-10%, because lease-up fees, maintenance markups, and misc charges add roughly 3-5 points to the effective rate. Worked example from those sources: $2,000/mo rent at 10% = $2,400/yr base + $750/yr amortized lease-up (0.5 turnovers x $1,500) + $450/yr maintenance markup ($3,000 repairs x 15%) = $3,600/yr = effective 15% of gross rent. Turnover frequency is the biggest swing factor: a placement fee of 70-100% of one month&#039;s rent equals 6-8% of a full year&#039;s gross by itself in a turnover year. These are practitioner/blog estimates, not government statistics - present them as &#039;typically 12-20% all-in, first year highest.&#039;</p>
+  </div>
+</section><section>
