+What a management company actually does, and what software covers
+The honest version of this list is the whole decision. Software now runs the office half of the job. The physical half (showings, inspections, being reachable) stays with you or a per-task vendor.
+Task
+Can software do it?
+Advertise and list vacant units (syndication to Zillow, Apartments.com, etc.)
+Mostly - software pushes one listing to the major portals automatically; photos and pricing judgment remain human
+Schedule and run showings
+Partially - scheduling tools and self-showing lockboxes handle booking; someone local still opens doors or checks the property after
+Tenant screening (credit, criminal, eviction history, income verification)
+Mostly - TransUnion SmartMove-class reports run instantly inside software; the accept/reject decision and consistent criteria are the owner's
+Lease preparation and signing
+Mostly - state-specific templates plus e-signature; a local attorney review of the base lease is a one-time cost, not a monthly one
+Move-in / move-out inspections and condition documentation
+No - requires physical presence; apps only standardize the checklist and photo record
+Rent collection
+Fully - ACH/card autopay, tenant portal, automatic receipts and ledger posting; 78-86% of renters prefer paying online anyway (Buildium/AppFolio)
+Late-payment follow-up, reminders, and late fees
+Fully - automatic reminders before due date, auto-assessed late fees, escalating notices without the owner touching it
+Bookkeeping, owner statements, and tax-time reporting
+Mostly - every payment and expense posts to the ledger automatically with Schedule E-ready exports; a CPA still files
+Maintenance request intake and dispatch
+Mostly - tenants submit requests with photos through a portal, software routes to the assigned vendor and tracks status; the owner still needs vendor relationships and approves big-ticket work
+24/7 emergency response
+Partially - intake, triage rules, and vendor routing are automatable; a human plumber still has to show up at 2am
+Vendor management (COIs, invoices, 1099s)
+Partially - software tracks documents, invoices, and issues 1099s; finding and keeping good vendors is human work
+Routine property inspections / drive-bys
+No - physical task; software only schedules and documents it
+Lease renewals and rent increases
+Mostly - renewal offers, market-rent comps, and e-sign renewals are automated; the pricing call is the owner's
+Security deposit handling and itemized deductions
+Mostly - escrow tracking, deadlines, and itemization letters are automated; state-specific rules still need to be set correctly once
+Evictions and court appearances
+No - attorney and court process; software's contribution is a clean payment ledger and notice trail that wins cases
+Fair housing / legal compliance
+Partially - software enforces identical screening criteria and documented processes, which is most of fair-housing defense; knowing local law is not automatable
+Where the rent sits each month
+With a management company, rent lands in the manager's trust account. You get the remainder after fees on their statement schedule, usually mid-month or later, and repair costs come out before you see them, with any markup already applied. Most owner complaints about managers are some version of this paragraph: slow statements, unclear deductions, surprises at payout time.
+Self-managing with software inverts that. Rent goes from the tenant's bank to yours through the payment processor, every charge and payment sits in a ledger you can open any hour, and repair invoices are numbers you approved rather than line items you discover. RenPro's books update with every payment, and the built-in AI answers questions about any number on the statement because it reads the same ledger.
+When hiring a Myrtle Beach management company still makes sense
+This page argues for self-managing with software because the math usually favors it. Not always. The honest exceptions:
+Out-of-state and remote owners: maintenance and emergency response is the single hardest thing to do from hundreds of miles away; a local manager provides around-the-clock physical response and an existing vendor network (Redfin, 'Managing a Rental Property Out of State'; Baselane, 'How to Manage Out-of-State Rental Property'). For short-term rentals managed from out of state, the guidance is that hiring a manager is almost always advisable despite 25-40% fees.
+Scale: the Census/HUD 2021 Rental Housing Finance Survey shows professional management climbing from ~22% of 1-4 unit properties to 84% of 150+ unit properties - past a certain door count you are hiring management labor either way, whether it's a PM firm or your own staff.
+Eviction-heavy or heavily regulated markets: an average eviction runs $3,500 and can reach $10,000+ (TransUnion SmartMove; Snappt), and procedural mistakes restart the clock and burn additional months of rent (~$2,540 average lost rent). A PM firm with an attorney pipeline and process discipline earns its fee if evictions are a recurring reality in your market.
+Owners with no time or high-value time: Census-derived data (iPropertyManagement) shows roughly 1 in 6 landlord-managed properties consumes 20-79 hours/month. If your property is a high-touch building or your working hour is worth more than the fee, 8-12% is a rational trade.
+Honest caveat that cuts both ways: UK survey data (Pegasus Insight, 2026) found landlords' reported time commitment was broadly similar whether or not they used an agent - hiring a PM buys execution, not zero involvement; the owner still oversees, approves, and audits.
